{"seconds": 80.8, "exit": 0, "final": "After **Nxf7 Kxf7 Qxf6+ Kg8**, White is up a knight (won the f7 pawn and f6 knight but lost nothing). White has multiple checks and winning positions.\n\nSo the main continuation seems to be: **1. Nxf7** and then:\n- **1...Bxh6 2. Nxd8** wins the queen\n- **1...Kxf7 2. Qxf6+ Kg8** (or other moves) and White is winning with a big attack\n\nNow let me write this up for the coaching note.\n\n---\n\n**Best move:** Nxf7\n\n**Explanation:** This knight move forks the king and queen. Black faces a critical choice:\n\n1. If Black captures with **Bxh6**, White recaptures with **Nxd8**, winning the queen outright.\n2. If Black plays **Kxf7**, White continues with **Qxf6+**, winning the knight and mounting a devastating attack on the exposed king.\n\n**Key lesson for 1500-rated players:** When your opponent's pieces are crowded (especially the king, queen, and bishop on the back rank), look for knight forks\u2014they often force immediate material loss or a winning attack. The f7 square is a particularly weak point to target near the enemy king.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nxf7\", \"line\": [\"Nxf7\", \"Bxh6\", \"Nxd8\", \"Rxd8\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\", \"attraction\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 13"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 13"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 13"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 13", "move": "Qxf8+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8/2NBP3/PPP2PPP/R3K2R w KQ - 3 13", "move": "Qh8+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "r1bqrbk1/pp3p2/2n1pn1Q/3p2N1/8